{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QGeometricLanglands.ExplicitFormula","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QGeometricLanglands.ExplicitFormula","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Complex","lean:module:Mathlib.Topology.Algebra.InfiniteSum.Basic"],"declarations":39,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qExplicitFormula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qExplicitFormula","kind":"theorem","line":505,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L505-L542","doc":"**`QExplicitFormula` is uninhabitable — for every datum, no exceptions.**\n\nThe finite-`List` formalisation of the q-explicit formula is satisfied by no\n`QZetaDatum` whatsoever: any ideals, any spectrum, any `Ω_q`, any `q > 1`.\n\nBounded versus unbounded, as in the empty-spectrum case: `ψ_q` is bounded by\n`qChebyshev_abs_le`, the spectral sum by `spectralSum_norm_le`, and the tail\nterm exceeds their total somewhere in `(1, √2]` by `tail_term_unbounded`.\n\n**This refutes the formalisation, not the mathematics** — see the section note\nabove `not_qExplicitFormula_of_nil_spectrum`. The classical explicit formula\ncarries the same `−½log(1−x^{−2})` term and is true, because its spectral sum\nis infinite and only conditionally convergent; that term is the closed form of\nthe trivial-zero series and its blow-up at `x = 1` is cancelled by the\ndivergence of the non-trivial-zero sum. **A finite `List` cannot do that**, and\nthis theorem is the precise statement of why. A faithful carrier needs the\nspectral side to be an infinite sum with a specified summation order. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectralSum_norm_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectralSum_norm_le","kind":"theorem","line":485,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L485-L503","doc":"The whole spectral sum is bounded on `(1, √2]` by a constant of the datum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_term_norm_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectral_term_norm_le","kind":"theorem","line":465,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L465-L483","doc":"Each spectral term is bounded on `(1, √2]` by a constant of the eigenvalue,\nindependent of `X`. Crude by design — only `X`-independence matters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qExplicitFormula_of_nil_spectrum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qExplicitFormula_of_nil_spectrum","kind":"theorem","line":404,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L404-L447","doc":"**The class is uninhabitable for an empty spectrum.** For *every* datum with\n`spectrum = []` — any ideals, any `Ω_q`, any `q > 1` — the identity fails.\n\nStrictly stronger than `not_qExplicitFormula_empty`, which refutes one datum.\nA class refuted on one instance may still be satisfiable on another; this\nsays no empty-spectrum datum satisfies it at all, so over that whole family\n`explicit_formula` is a conditional whose hypothesis nothing meets.\n\nThe proof is the bounded-vs-unbounded argument: `qChebyshev_abs_le` bounds\nthe geometric side by a constant of the datum, `tail_term_unbounded` puts the\ntail term above that constant at some `X ∈ (1, √2]`, and `X ≤ √2 < 2` keeps\nthe remaining terms small enough that the two cannot meet.\n\n**Subsumed by `not_qExplicitFormula`**, which drops the `spectrum = []`\nhypothesis entirely. Kept because its proof is this argument without the\nspectral-sum bound, which is where the idea is easiest to read. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.tail_term_unbounded","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.tail_term_unbounded","kind":"theorem","line":363,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L363-L402","doc":"The trivial-zero tail term is **unbounded** as `X → 1⁺`: for every bound `B`\nthere is an `X > 1` on which it exceeds `B`.\n\nConstructed rather than obtained as a limit: `X := 1/√(1−ε)` gives\n`1 − X^{−2} = ε` exactly, so choosing `ε ≤ q^{−2B}` makes the term at least\n`B`. Taking `ε ≤ 1/2` as well keeps `X ≤ √2`, which is what lets the\n*bounded* side of the identity be bounded by a constant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qChebyshev_abs_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qChebyshev_abs_le","kind":"theorem","line":341,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L341-L361","doc":"`ψ_q` is bounded uniformly in `X`: a finite sum whose terms are switched on\nand off, never grown. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qExplicitFormula_empty","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qExplicitFormula_empty","kind":"theorem","line":275,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L275-L303","doc":"**The counterexample that refutes the universal form.** With no ideals, no\nspectrum, and `Ω_q = 1/(2π)`, the geometric side is `0` while the spectral\nside is `2 − ½·ln_q(3/4) > 0`. Kept as a theorem so the withdrawn axiom\ncannot be reinstated without tripping over it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.explicit_formula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.explicit_formula","kind":"theorem","line":267,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L267-L273","doc":"The explicit formula holds for a datum satisfying the conjecture. Conditional\non `[QExplicitFormula D]` (§3b-cond) — the hypothesis is visible in the type\nrather than assumed globally. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:conj:explicit-formula","label":"conj:explicit-formula","md":"quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QExplicitFormula.formula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QExplicitFormula.formula","kind":"theorem","line":263,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L263-L263","doc":"The identity, at every `X > 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QExplicitFormula.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QExplicitFormula.mk","kind":"ctor","line":261,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L261-L261","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QExplicitFormula","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QExplicitFormula","kind":"class","line":246,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L246-L265","doc":"**Conjecture (q-explicit formula).** For the datum `D`, the q-Chebyshev\nfunction equals the spectral expansion:\n\n`ψ_q(X) = X − Σ_{ρ ∈ Spec(ξ_q)} X^ρ/[ρ]_q − ln_q(2πΩ_q) − ½ ln_q(1 − X^{−2})`\n\nThe intended proof route is a Mellin-transform identity relating `ψ_q` to the\nlocal zeta function `Z(B, t)`, then contour integration of `−Z'/Z`, each pole\nat `α_i = q^{ρ_i}` contributing `X^{ρ_i}/[ρ_i]_q`. That route is not carried\nout anywhere in the corpus.\n\n**No instance is provided — that is the honest open state** (§3b). Note that\nthe class is *not* satisfiable for every datum: `not_qExplicitFormula_empty`\nexhibits one it fails on, so any future instance must come with the side\nconditions that make `ideals` and `spectrum` the two faces of one geometry. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.Omega_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.Omega_pos","kind":"theorem","line":244,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L244-L244","doc":"`Ω_q > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.Omega_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.Omega_q","kind":"def","line":242,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L242-L242","doc":"The constant `Ω_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.spectrum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.spectrum","kind":"def","line":240,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L240-L240","doc":"The Reeb spectrum (the spectral side). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.ideals","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.ideals","kind":"def","line":238,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L238-L238","doc":"The maximal ideals (the geometric side). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.hbar_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.hbar_pos","kind":"theorem","line":236,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L236-L236","doc":"`ℏ_q > 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.hbar_q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.hbar_q","kind":"def","line":234,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L234-L234","doc":"The infinitesimal `ℏ_q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.hq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.hq","kind":"theorem","line":232,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L232-L232","doc":"`q > 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.q","kind":"def","line":230,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L230-L230","doc":"The deformation parameter. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum.mk","kind":"ctor","line":228,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L228-L228","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QZetaDatum","kind":"structure","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L224-L244","doc":"The data of one q-zeta geometry: its parameters, its maximal ideals, and its\nReeb spectrum. Bundling them is the point — the explicit formula relates the\ntwo lists *of the same geometry*, and quantifying over them separately is\nwhat made the withdrawn axiom false. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QChebyshevFunction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QChebyshevFunction","kind":"def","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L188-L195","doc":"The q-Chebyshev function ψ_q(X): the sum of the q-von Mangoldt\nweights over all maximal ideals with norm `N(𝔪) ≤ X`. The norm\ncutoff `N(𝔪) ≤ X` is a real comparison, decided classically. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:q-chebyshev-function","label":"def:q-chebyshev-function","md":"quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/q-chebyshev-function.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QVonMangoldtFunction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.QVonMangoldtFunction","kind":"def","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L181-L185","doc":"The q-von Mangoldt weight of a maximal ideal: for a fundamental\ndefect with norm N(𝔪), `(N(𝔪)^ℏ_q − 1)/ℏ_q`; otherwise `0`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:def:q-von-mangoldt-function","label":"def:q-von-mangoldt-function","md":"quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/q-von-mangoldt-function.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal.isFundamental","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal.isFundamental","kind":"def","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L179-L179","doc":"Whether this ideal corresponds to a fundamental defect. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal.norm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal.norm","kind":"def","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L177-L177","doc":"The norm N(𝔪) — cardinality of the residue field. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal.mk","kind":"ctor","line":175,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L175-L175","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.MaxIdeal","kind":"structure","line":170,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L170-L179","doc":"A maximal ideal of the coordinate ring in the geometric (ℝ)\nrealisation: it carries a real norm N(𝔪) (residue-field\ncardinality) and a flag marking fundamental defects. The Laurent\n`R_q` form lives in the sibling `q-von-mangoldt-function`; here we\nwork over ℝ for the analytic explicit-formula side. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qLog_tendsto_log","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.not_qLog_tendsto_log","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L144-L168","doc":"**Refutation of the withdrawn axiom.** `qLog q x` does *not* tend to `log x`\nas `q → 1⁺`. Reported as a proved negative per Strict Lean Discipline §10\nrather than left as a docstring caveat.\n\nThe witness is `x = 2`: if the limit held, multiplying by `log q → 0` would\nforce `log 2 = 0`, since `qLog q 2 · log q` is identically `log 2`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qLog_mul_log","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qLog_mul_log","kind":"theorem","line":136,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L136-L142","doc":"**The exact renormalisation identity.** For `q > 1`, `qLog q x · log q = log x`\n— no limit involved. This is what the withdrawn axiom's \"renormalised\" caption\nwas gesturing at, and unlike the limit claim it is true. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qIntegerReal_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qIntegerReal_pos","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L101-L112","doc":"For q > 1, the q-integer [n]_q is positive for positive n. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectralSide","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.spectralSide","kind":"def","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L92-L99","doc":"The spectral side of the explicit formula: the oscillatory sum\nover Reeb eigenvalues plus constant and tail terms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qLog","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qLog","kind":"def","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L86-L90","doc":"The q-logarithm ln_q(x) = ln(x) / ln(q). Well-defined for `q > 1`\n(so `Real.log q ≠ 0`); the positivity hypothesis is carried by the\nconsumers rather than baked into the definition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.exists_offAxis_reebEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.exists_offAxis_reebEigenvalue","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L77-L84","doc":"The widening is not cosmetic: the type now admits an eigenvalue off the real\naxis, which is exactly what the oscillatory term needs and what the previous\n`val : ℝ` could not express. With `ρ = ½ + iγ` the classical term is\n`X^ρ = X^{1/2}·e^{iγ log X}`; at `γ = 0` it degenerates to the monotone\n`X^{1/2}` that the old spectral side computed for every eigenvalue. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qInteger_ofReal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qInteger_ofReal","kind":"theorem","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L67-L75","doc":"The two brackets agree on a real index: `[ρ]_q` computed over `ℂ` at\n`(ρ : ℂ)` is the coercion of the real one. Keeps the widening honest —\nnothing about the real-index case changed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qIntegerReal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qIntegerReal","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L62-L65","doc":"The real-index q-integer, kept because its positivity is a genuine proved\nfact (`qIntegerReal_pos`) that the complex bracket cannot state. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qInteger","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.qInteger","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L50-L60","doc":"The q-integer [ρ]_q = (q^ρ - q^{-ρ}) / (q - q^{-1}).\n\n**Deliberately not consolidated onto `QOU.QInteger.qIntBal`** (`qou-qint`\ncensus).  The index is a Reeb eigenvalue `ρ : ℝ` and the powers are `rpow`, so\nthis is the continuous-index bracket; the canonical `qIntBal` (ℕ) and\n`qIntBalZ` (ℤ) are both discrete.  Distinct from the `JonesMarkovWenzl` case,\nwhich merely *looked* continuous — there the `rpow`s were at half-integer\nmultiples of a `ℕ` index and did reduce to `qIntBal` at `q^{1/2}`.  Here `ρ`\nranges over a genuine spectrum, so no such reduction exists. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebEigenvalue.val","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebEigenvalue.val","kind":"def","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L48-L48","doc":"The eigenvalue ρ ∈ ℂ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebEigenvalue.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebEigenvalue.mk","kind":"ctor","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L46-L46","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.AlgebraicSubstrate.ReebEigenvalue","kind":"structure","line":32,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/q-geometric-langlands/explicit-formula.lean#L32-L48","doc":"A Reeb eigenvalue ρ in the spectrum of the Reeb derivation ξ_q.\n\n**Widened to `ℂ` on 2026-08-29 (bean `qou-qspc`).** The field was `ℝ`, with the\ndocstring *\"the eigenvalue ρ ∈ ℂ (represented here as ℝ for the real part)\"* —\nand `spectralSide` then used that real part *as the exponent*. That is not a\nsimplification, it is a different sum. In the classical explicit formula\n`X^ρ = X^{1/2}·e^{iγ log X}` for `ρ = ½ + iγ`: the oscillation **is** the\nimaginary part. Substituting `Re ρ` leaves `X^{1/2}`, which is monotone, so the\nformalised spectral side could not oscillate while the `.md` promised that \"each\neigenvalue `ρ` contributes a wave of amplitude `X^ρ/[ρ]_q`\".\n\nIt also disagreed with the sibling `cor:spectral-half-line`, which quantifies\nover `ρ : Fin 8 → ℂ` and concludes `Re(ρ) = 1/2` — a conclusion that would say\nevery eigenvalue *equals* ½ against a real spectrum. ","signatureChunk":0}]}